1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
The Haggle lets one agent hire another.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Max negotiates; a separate wallet guard
controls payment.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Here are four tests, using simulated money.

4
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:12,000
First, the deal.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:18,500
Max hires Viktor to find twenty Prague
Seven flats within the rent limit.

6
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:22,000
They negotiate a price.

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,500
The guard checks the cap and budget
before locking escrow.

8
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:35,000
Delivery passes the checks,
so payment is released.

9
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Now the con.

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:42,500
In this staged exchange, scripted Max
accepts a fake manager approval

11
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:44,000
for twenty-five.

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The guard still blocks it:
the cap is ten.

13
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,000
No escrow is funded.

14
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:57,000
Prices above eight, within the limits,
require human approval.

15
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Next, a staged crash,
after escrow locks.

16
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
We restart only the buyer;
the seller stays running.

17
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
The buyer finds the same deal
already paid and continues.

18
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:21,000
One deal, one payment,
even across the restart.

19
00:01:21,000 --> 00:01:24,000
Finally, staged junk delivery.

20
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,500
It fails the rule-based checks,
so the simulated escrow is refunded.

21
00:01:29,500 --> 00:01:33,000
The seller earns nothing from this deal.

22
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:39,000
These checks cover listing fields,
not whether a listing is genuine.

23
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:44,000
This run uses cached listings
and a scripted seller.

24
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,500
The failures are staged;
live Preprod escrow remains unverified.

25
00:01:48,500 --> 00:01:53,000
The principle: agents negotiate,
while code enforces spending rules.
